{
  "_comment": "Limits for diff.js. diffPercent is % of compared (unmasked) pixels that differ, per region name or kind (chrome is stricter than text). inkPercent is differing pixels / pixels that are ink in either image, per region. flatDeltaE is the CIE76 colour difference between a region's dominant colours (wrong greys). blurMae is the mean absolute error of blurred images, % of full scale. shadowError is RMSE in luminance levels. sizeDelta is the window size difference in CSS px. rowYOffset is the mean vertical offset of text rows in CSS px. Per-case entries in \"cases\" override the defaults key by key. These start loose so crude fixtures pass or fail meaningfully; tighten as the module improves. macos-light, macos-dark, linux-light and linux-dark (built skins) are held to the tightened limits, and so are windows-light and windows-dark (measured on the Windows runner, where the reference has ClearType text and the rendering has grayscale, so the text ink limits are looser than macOS's). windows-dark's toolbars have two surfaces of almost the same area (the Mica navigation row and the command bar), so its flat colour is a near tie: a change that flips it shows as a large flatDeltaE without a real colour error. macos-{light,dark}-{text,code} (TextEdit, built) are held to tightened limits too, measured on the macOS runner (text ink is looser there than on a developer machine: glyph antialiasing differs a little). The two code captures have a deeper window shadow than the text captures and the folder captures (a capture-state difference of macOS, not something the CSS can match for both): their shadowError limits are looser (about 9) than the text cases' (under 3); every other metric is as tight as the text cases. windows-light-browser and windows-dark-browser (Edge, measured on the Windows runner) are held to tightened limits too; the dark case's rowYOffset is loose (48) on purpose: the capture's page scrollbar is dark (Edge's page follows the dark theme) while the rendered iframe's is light (a sandboxed srcdoc page keeps its own light colour scheme, which CSS outside the frame cannot change), and the dark track is ink down the whole height that merges the reference's four text rows into one band, so the row comparison is meaningless there. windows-light-list and windows-dark-list are hand-written fixtures of a view authors cannot pick from the editor (the QA harness has no way to author a real list-view capture yet); they are marked informational: reported in the table but excluded from the pass/fail exit code.",
